  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 42a7ef911f4c60e408989a759d2b9b18, type: 3}
  m_Name: PY_LISTS
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::CodeEscape.Programming.LessonData
  id: PY_LISTS
  language: 1
  conceptTitle: Lists and Indexes
  symbol: '[ ]'
  relatedQuestionIds:
  - PY_09_LISTS
  - HZ_PY_05_LISTS
  - PY_RUN_05_LISTS
  - PY_RUN_12_LISTS_APPEND
  topics:
  - Lists
  definition: A list stores several values, in order, inside one variable.
  howItWorks: Write a list with square brackets and commas between the items. Each
    item has a position number called an index. Indexes start at 0, so the first
    item is list[0], the second is list[1], and so on.
  exampleCode: 'snacks = ["apple", "chips", "grapes", "nuts"]

    print(snacks[0])

    print(snacks[3])'
  exampleExplanation: snacks[0] is the first item, apple. snacks[3] is the fourth
    item, nuts, because counting starts at 0. Asking for snacks[4] would cause an
    IndexError, because there is no fifth item.
  tip: len(snacks) tells you how many items a list has - here it is 4.
